<section class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-signal-700">Home Station</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700">A home station can use practically any 2M or dual-band transceiver, paired with a Terminal Node Controller (TNC) to convert AX.25 packet data for your computer. Kantronics has long been the go-to manufacturer for APRS-focused TNCs, used both at mountaintop digipeater sites and on the desktop. Tune the radio to the US national simplex frequency, 144.390 MHz, connect it to a properly configured TNC, and run APRS software such as UI-View32, PinPointAPRS, Xastir, or SARTrack.</p>
  {{ solutionPhotos.render(homeStationPhotos) }}
</section>

<section class="mb-10">
  <h2 class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-signal-700">Mountaintop Digipeater</h2>
  <p class="mt-2 text-slate-700">For years, a typical mountaintop APRS digipeater was built from a 2M mobile radio (amateur or commercial), a Kantronics KPC-3 TNC, a power supply with backup power, a bandpass filter tuned to 144.390 MHz, coax, and a rugged 2M antenna capable of surviving harsh winter weather. Much of that equipment is still in use today, with the main addition being an internet connection so signals heard at the site can be uploaded to APRS-IS. One example is the Lookout Pass APRS digipeater, along I-90 at the Idaho/Montana state line. The site is owned by the Mac Pass Repeater Group (MPRG); initial work was completed by Bill, K7CTS (SK), Jim, WA7RVV (SK), and Dave, K7GPS. The site originally ran on a 2x6 ft solar panel until ground power was run the following year, and now has full-time utility power alongside a number of other amateur radio installations.</p>
  {{ solutionPhotos.render(mountaintopPhotos) }}
</section>
